Article 18
(1) Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 17 shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. population and civil registration;
f. law;
g. women's empowerment and child protection;
h. cooperatives and micro enterprises;
i. mental and spiritual; and
j. public peace and order.
k. community and pekon empowerment.
(2) Prevention of acts of violence by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and sustainable manner based on RPJPD and RPJMD.

Article 14
(1) The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 13 letter a include:
a. establishing policies for the protection, empowerment of women and children;
b. implementing policies, protection, and empowerment of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children, in accordance with regional financial capacity;
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children; and
g. realizing a Child-Friendly Regency.
(2) In order to implement the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes programs and activities for the protection and empowerment of women and children in the Regional Action Plan as a basis for Regional Apparatus in implementing the protection and empowerment of women and children.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) constitutes part of the RPJMD.
(4) Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 17
(1) To prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government conducts empowerment and builds awareness among families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling and realizing a Child-Friendly Regency.
(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government undertakes efforts as follows:
a. providing material on the prevention of acts of violence against women and children in formal, non-formal, and informal education;
b. provision of accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;

Article 29
(1) Funding for protection of women and children victims of violence includes:
a. medical services, which include doctor examinations, treatment costs, hospital costs, medication costs and medical support costs;
b. medicolegal services, which include examinations for Visum et Repertum and Visum et Psikiatrikum;
c. psychosocial services, which consist of counseling and psychological therapy as well as safe houses (shelters);
d. legal services;
e. economic empowerment, in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access.
(2) Funding management is carried out through technical institutions appointed by the Regional Government, and is conducted transparently and can be legally accounted for.

Article 25
(1) Regional Governments establish partnerships with the business world in the protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(2) Partnerships as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out through:
   a. notification of employment opportunity information for women and children victims of acts of violence;
   b. education and training for women and children victims of acts of violence;
   c. development of facilities and infrastructure related to the protection of women and children from acts of violence.

